ad  of everyone else but always getting back  to that that old-time message of what  the good news of Christ is could it be  that Billy Graham had an iPhone that  would be nice to know I would not be  surprised you know you he once said that  one of his big regrets if he had to talk  about regrets in life was that he  crossed the line into politics he was so  revered and admired on both sides of the  aisle why do you think he regretted that  well because he looked back at the tapes  water Watergate his close association  with Richard Nixon which I think was  more personal than it was political but  I think later he looked back and so how  those those lines can become blurred and  people can easily confuse a partisan  political agenda for the kingdom of God  that of course transcends all of that  and so Billy Graham's primary goal was  to reach people all people with the  message of God's love of the Day of  Judgment that's coming for all people  and of the freedom that comes through  forgiveness through through the cross  and so he he I think more than made up  for whatever errors he might have made  in his past Christ's message is love thy  neighbor don't judge don't be prideful  when you listen to political talk today  whether it's by our officials or online  you know that is does not exist does  that argue for a Graham like figure to  jump into politics to lift it up or to  stay way away because none of Christ's  message can be found in it well we need  people in politics who of course are  have consciences that are guided by by  morality and by the Spirit of God but I  think we also need people are standing  outside of politics speaking a word of  grace and the redemption and of coming  judgment and I think that's what Graham  was able to do he was able to say well  we all know where we're sinners we know  that no matter how much we try to say  everything's relative and everything's  fine we know that's not true in our own  hearts at the same time he loved people  so Billy Graham was never angry Billy  Graham was never resentful he was never  warring against the culture in the world  around and he was John 3:16 he being  loved the world because God loved the  world enough to say come to Christ I saw  the time I saw an old interview that  Diane Sawyer had done with him years ago  and he said when the time came he  doesn't want people to say nice things  about him because he said he didn't  deserve it what he wants hear from the  Lord is well done well done my servant  you know him personally what stands out  to you about him what stood out to me  was the gravity first time I met him I  was in my early twenties very young in  ministry and walking into the room it  was almost like coming into the room  with an Old Testament prophet in one  sense but at the same time there was  this warmth  and so I think of him sort of like I  heard someone mention a preacher in  London many years ago and said he was a  steamroller but a gentle steamroller and  I think that was true of Billy Graham he  had a gentleness and a warmth but the  gravity of the message was was  overwhelming and still endurance we need  that message these days too he has had a  profound impact and over the past  century thank you thank you very much  Rusty Moore hey Russell Moore thank you
 

